          "goal": "Vulnerability analysis of External AMM pools and routers",
          "focus_areas": [
            "Trust boundaries and assumptions when interacting with external AMM pools and routers",
            "Pool balance, reserve, and LP-token accounting used by vaults or oracles",
            "Slippage, deadline, callback, and recipient handling across swaps and liquidity operations",
            "Validation of pool identities, token ordering, and externally supplied parameters",
            "Reentrancy, token transfer behavior, and adverse effects from non-standard ERC-20 or pool implementations"
          ],
          "priority": 8,
          "reasoning": "WHY NOW: This is an unvisited major component at a critical external-system boundary, and the architecture shows SwapRouter, Curve, Maverick, Balancer, vaults, and oracles depending on external pool behavior. It can expose incorrect pricing, token accounting, routing, or liquidity-removal assumptions with potentially high financial impact. EXIT CRITERIA: Review all integration paths and call flows involving external AMM pools and routers; identify whether pool identities, tokens, amounts, slippage, recipients, callbacks, and accounting are safely constrained; document any concrete exploitable issue or conclude that no vulnerability is supported by the static code.",
          "category": "aspect",
          "expected_impact": "high"
        }
      ]
    },
